    {
      "heading": "Not always a whole number",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Sides 5 and 9. 25 plus 81 is 106.",
          "write": "c² = 25 + 81 = 106"
        },
        {
          "say": "Root 106 is an exact answer. It's a surd.",
          "write": "c = √106 m"
        },
        {
          "say": "Or round it. About 10.3 metres.",
          "write": "√106 ≈ 10.3 m"
        },
        {
          "say": "A TV is 80 by 45. The diagonal is root 8425, about 91.8.",
          "write": "√(6400 + 2025) ≈ 91.8 cm",
          "diagram": "diagrams/y8/maths/pythagoras-theorem-ex3.svg",
          "alt": "A TV screen 80 cm wide and 45 cm high with its diagonal marked"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Triples and the converse",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Some whole numbers fit exactly. 3, 4, 5. 5, 12, 13. 8, 15, 17.",
          "write": "3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17"
        },
        {
          "say": "Multiples work too. 6, 8, 10.",
          "write": "6-8-10"
        },
        {
          "say": "Is 8, 12, 15 right-angled? 64 plus 144 is 208.",
          "write": "8² + 12² = 208"
        },
        {
          "say": "15 squared is 225. Not equal, so it's not right-angled.",
          "write": "208 ≠ 225  ✗"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Watch out for these",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Square first, then add. Not 6 plus 8, squared.",
          "write": "✗ (6 + 8)² = 196"
        },
        {
          "say": "Root of 36 plus 64 is 10, not 14. The root doesn't split.",
          "write": "√(36 + 64) ≠ 6 + 8"
        },
        {
          "say": "Don't forget the square root at the end.",
          "write": "c² = 100 → c = 10"
        }
      ]
    },
